WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.120
Every year hundreds of thousands&nbsp;
of horseshoe crabs arrive on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.240
beaches of the Atlantic coast&nbsp;
of America to lay their eggs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.960
And every year, hundreds of thousands&nbsp;
of horseshoe crabs are rounded up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.080
brought to the lab - not to be killed, but&nbsp;
for their blood to be carefully extracted.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.840
These animals, often called living fossils, are&nbsp;
one of the ‘oldest’ creatures on the planet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.920
They have remained nearly unchanged since they&nbsp;
first appeared on earth over 450 million years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:42.400
ago. This is due to some exceptionally effective&nbsp;
adaptations, and genes that code for remarkable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.520
molecules that have allowed the horseshoe&nbsp;
crab to survive, just as it is, for so long.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.360
One of these ancient compounds is the reason&nbsp;
that hordes of these animals are dredged up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.320
from the ocean, jabbed with a hypodermic&nbsp;
needle, and their blue blood drained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:05.520
processed, and sold. Their blood, made blue from&nbsp;
a copper-based oxygen-carrying molecule, is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:11.280
valuable that it is the basis for a multi-million&nbsp;
dollar pharmaceutical industry. So valuable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.600
that a single liter of it goes for around $16,000&nbsp;
- one of the most valuable liquids on earth.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.960
Our reliance on these animals puts&nbsp;
immense pressure on a fragile ecosystem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.560
and so far, scientists have struggled to&nbsp;
recreate this compound and its effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.120
in the lab. What is it about this&nbsp;
primitive compound that we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.680
so badly, and why can we only seem&nbsp;
to get it from this one creature?

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.360
The American horseshoe crab (on screen: Limulus&nbsp;
polyphemus) is an ancient, aquatic arthropod.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:49.360
They belong to their own class of animals, called&nbsp;
Merostomata, and are not actually crabs. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.480
more closely related to scorpions, with their&nbsp;
predecessors diverging from their arachnid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:01.280
cousins around 480 million years ago. Some&nbsp;
recent studies even suggest they are arachnids.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:07.280
The modern horseshoe crab as we know it has&nbsp;
technically only been around for 20 million years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:13.920
but some of its early relatives, like Limulus&nbsp;
darwini, existed around 150 million years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.920
and look nearly indistinguishable&nbsp;
from today’s horseshoe crabs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:24.960
The iconic body plan has been around even longer,&nbsp;
emerging around 450 million years ago. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.480
changes within the horseshoe crab group have been&nbsp;
shockingly minor in the big picture of evolution.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.880
Here’s some perspective on just how long ago&nbsp;
horseshoe crabs came into existence. Pangea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:43.440
the supercontinent of the past formed 335&nbsp;
million years ago and began to break apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:51.360
about 175 million years ago. The non-avian&nbsp;
dinosaurs emerged 245 million years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:58.240
and were wiped out 66 million years ago. The earth&nbsp;
descended into, and emerged from, 2 completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:04.400
different ice ages since these crabs came about.&nbsp;
The world has changed so much since then. And all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.600
the while horseshoe crabs have been here, crawling&nbsp;
along the seafloor, standing the test of time.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.760
Some of the reason natural&nbsp;
selection has preserved them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.640
pretty much as they are, is their hardy body plan.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:23.440
Their hard shell, called a carapace, is an&nbsp;
exoskeleton so strong that only sharks or turtles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:30.000
can penetrate it. And guiding them through the&nbsp;
ocean depths are 9 eyes - 2 compound eyes which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.760
act much like our own eyes, 5 secondary simple&nbsp;
eyes on top of their shell which can detect UV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:41.120
light, and 2 ventral eyes located on their&nbsp;
underside, perhaps to help with orientation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:48.240
Along with their complex circulatory system, 5&nbsp;
pairs of gills, and 12 bristled legs, evolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.360
created them to be creatures extremely well&nbsp;
adapted to their particular environmental niche.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.720
But beyond the physical traits that we can&nbsp;
observe, much of their survival is due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:04.720
something we can’t see - their incredible, but&nbsp;
simple immune system. It’s protected them as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:10.960
species from bacterial infection for eons. It&nbsp;
works in an entirely different way from ours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.440
and in the late 1960s, we began to&nbsp;
harness its power for ourselves.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.160
In 1968, two researchers at the&nbsp;
Marine Biological Laboratory in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.400
Massachusetts observed that blood cells&nbsp;
from horseshoe crabs vigorously clot in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.480
the presence of bacterial endotoxin.&nbsp;
When they published their paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.280
they had no idea that what they found would&nbsp;
revolutionise drug safety testing forever.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.680
Pretty much every creature in the&nbsp;
world is vulnerable to bacterial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:44.560
infection - and the horseshoe crab is&nbsp;
no exception. Once an infection begins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.400
bacteria can reproduce quickly, and many give off&nbsp;
toxins which damage specific tissues in the body.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:56.880
Botulism, for example, is an illness caused by a&nbsp;
neurotoxic protein produced by a bacteria called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.800
Clostridium botulinum. The toxin can affect&nbsp;
your nerves, paralyze you, and even kill you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.720
Toxins like this are called exotoxins. They&nbsp;
are released from live bacteria into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:13.760
surrounding environment during an infection.&nbsp;
But, bacteria don’t have to release these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:18.880
exotoxins in order to be dangerous. In&nbsp;
fact, they don’t even have to be alive.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.720
Once a bacteria is killed within the&nbsp;
body, they sometimes release endotoxins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.440
Endotoxins are the lipid portions of&nbsp;
lipopolysaccharides (LPSs) that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:35.280
part of the outer membrane of the cell wall of&nbsp;
some bacteria. The endotoxins are released when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:42.720
the bacteria die and the cell wall breaks apart.&nbsp;
This toxin is a pyrogen - a fever causing agent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.720
If it gets into the bloodstream, it can&nbsp;
lead to septic shock, and can be deadly.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.960
But, fighting off these types of&nbsp;
infections is what immune systems are for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.400
Immune systems have developed&nbsp;
to protect all different kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.720
of organisms from foreign pathogens.&nbsp;
And during the course of evolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.440
two different kinds of general immune system&nbsp;
emerged within multicellular organisms.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:09.440
Humans and many other vertebrates have adaptive&nbsp;
immune systems that protect us by strategically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.440
mounting a defense against invading bacteria.&nbsp;
It is activated by exposure to pathogens, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.240
uses an immune memory to learn about the threat&nbsp;
and enhance the immune response accordingly.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:26.800
But many invertebrates, including horseshoe&nbsp;
crabs, don’t have this adaptive immunity. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:32.480
they have an innate immune system, which attacks&nbsp;
based on the identification of general threat.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:37.280
The basis for a horseshoe crab’s immune&nbsp;
response are cells called granular amoebocytes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:41.200
When bacteria come into contact&nbsp;
with a horseshoe crab’s blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.440
they trigger an enzyme cascade,&nbsp;
mediated by these amoebocytes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:50.960
which causes the blood in the immediate area&nbsp;
of the infection to clot into a gel. The gel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:56.800
surrounds and isolates the infection from the rest&nbsp;
of the crab, and the pathogens are neutralized.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:59.520
The clotting from granular&nbsp;
amoebocytes is a simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.280
but very effective way for the horseshoe&nbsp;
crab to defend itself from infection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:10.000
And, as researchers began to realize in&nbsp;
the 1960s, it’s a very effective way for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:15.840
us to detect the presence of toxins in places&nbsp;
where we really, really don’t want them to be.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.960
When creating injectable healthcare products&nbsp;
like vaccines, medical implants, and IVs, it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.800
imperative that they are free of any invading&nbsp;
microbes. It’s easy enough to sterilize the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.920
solutions or devices by blasting them with heat,&nbsp;
radiation, or gas that is deadly to bacteria.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:42.240
But killing bacteria isn’t enough to make these&nbsp;
products safe. If certain bacteria was present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:48.880
before sterilization, the endotoxin will remain,&nbsp;
and can lead to severe consequences if injected.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:54.160
Historically, pharmaceutical companies&nbsp;
got around this problem with huge colonies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.840
of rabbits - needed for what’s&nbsp;
called the rabbit pyrogen test.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.920
To see if a product or drug is contaminated&nbsp;
with endotoxin, three (unlucky) rabbits would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:08.320
be injected with a small amount of the&nbsp;
drug or product in question and monitored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:14.960
for four hours. Rabbits have a similar pyrogen&nbsp;
tolerance to humans, so if any develop a fever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:20.240
the batch would be considered to be contaminated&nbsp;
with bacterial endotoxin. This is an effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:25.547
way of preventing endotoxins from accidentally&nbsp;
being injected into the public, but because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.547 --> 00:08:32.080
is an in-vivo test - meaning done inside a living&nbsp;
organism- it’s very time-consuming and expensive.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:36.080
So when researchers noticed the&nbsp;
clotting effect of the horseshoe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:40.080
crab’s amoebocytes in the presence of&nbsp;
endotoxin, they realized it could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.760
an in vitro way of spotting contamination&nbsp;
- a much cheaper, easier, and faster test.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:52.640
This in vitro test is called the LAL test&nbsp;
- limulus amoebocyte lysate. Limulus being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:59.040
limulus polyphemus, the american horseshoe crab.&nbsp;
This test has become the worldwide standard for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:05.120
screening for bacterial contamination. It is&nbsp;
capable of detecting endotoxin at significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:11.280
lower levels than the rabbit pyrogen test.&nbsp;
Today, every drug certified by the FDA must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:17.680
be tested using LAL, as do surgical implants&nbsp;
such as pacemakers and prosthetic devices.

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:20.720
After the horseshoe crabs are&nbsp;
brought to the lab, the tissue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:26.160
around their heart is pierced with a needle&nbsp;
and up to 30 percent of their blood is drained.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:30.640
The amoebocytes in the blood are then&nbsp;
extracted from that for the LAL test.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:37.120
Upon exposure to endotoxins, the&nbsp;
amebocytes undergo a rapid enzyme cascade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:40.000
that causes the cells to stick&nbsp;
together and form a thick clot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:45.200
This clot can form in around 90&nbsp;
seconds, giving a nearly instant result.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.240
We’ve never found anything that is as&nbsp;
sensitive in detecting endotoxin than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:55.440
the horseshoe crab’s amoebocytes. If there&nbsp;
are dangerous bacterial endotoxins—even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:00.400
at a concentration of one part per trillion&nbsp;
- a clot will form and can be detected.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:05.920
This is great news for us. Pretty much every&nbsp;
single person who has ever had an injection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:11.600
of any sort has been protected because of this&nbsp;
compound from this strange, ancient creature. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:16.960
only problem is that for this test to be readily&nbsp;
available, pharmaceutical companies need a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:23.040
supply of the blood of live crabs - which, as&nbsp;
you’d guess, is not such great news for the crabs.

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:30.000
In theory, the process of extracting blood&nbsp;
from the horseshoe crabs does not kill them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.520
It’s sort of like blood donation, albeit a&nbsp;
nonconsensual one. And once their blood is taken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:40.160
the crabs are released in a new location so they&nbsp;
do not accidentally get caught a second time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:44.400
ensuring they have a chance to recover.&nbsp;
Their blood volume rebounds in about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:49.120
week - and the LAL industry states that there&nbsp;
are no long term ill effects for the crabs.

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:55.840
They measured mortality rates of less than 3%.&nbsp;
But conservationists tell a different story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:01.040
Between 10 and 30 percent of the bled animals,&nbsp;
according to varying estimates, actually die.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:06.800
And 30% of the animals per year dying equates&nbsp;
to losses in the hundreds of thousands.

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:12.480
And this isn’t just bad for the horseshoe crab,&nbsp;
but for the entire ecosystem in which they live.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:17.280
Many other species of animals rely on&nbsp;
the horseshoe crabs’ eggs for food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:18.800
like shorebirds and turtles.

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:24.480
So the obvious question is - why haven’t&nbsp;
scientists made a synthetic alternative to LAL?

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:29.280
Since the 1970s they have certainly&nbsp;
been trying - and luckily for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:31.520
crabs they have started to have some success.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:38.400
In 1995, scientists from the National&nbsp;
University of Singapore were finally able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:44.480
identify and isolate the gene responsible for the&nbsp;
endotoxin-sensitive protein called Factor C – the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:50.480
most important component in the LAL test – and&nbsp;
produce it in yeast. Several years after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:56.160
they were able to create a rapid endotoxin&nbsp;
test based on this recombinant protein.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:01.440
But despite these advances, these synthetic&nbsp;
tests are still not widely available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:05.840
They have been adopted extremely slowly&nbsp;
due to regulatory and safety concerns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:12.240
Europe did not recognize the synthetic protein&nbsp;
as an alternate endotoxin detection until 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:18.080
and the FDA in the US did not approve the first&nbsp;
drug that used an endotoxin test based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:24.560
synthetic protein until 2018. And earlier this&nbsp;
year, the American Pharmacopeia, which sets the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:29.920
scientific standards for drugs and other products&nbsp;
in the U.S., declined to place the synthetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:34.960
protein on equal footing with crab lysate,&nbsp;
claiming that its safety is still unproven.

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:40.240
For now, we still need the horseshoe&nbsp;
crab and their baby blue blood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:44.320
But as more and more studies come out that&nbsp;
demonstrate the safety of the synthetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:49.600
version of the endotoxin test, the horseshoe&nbsp;
crabs can breathe a bit of a sigh of relief.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:54.400
While they still face threats from&nbsp;
overfishing for bait and habitat destruction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:58.560
the adoption of this technology will&nbsp;
relieve at least one major pressure.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.960
Our medical need for horseshoe crabs is what has&nbsp;
started to push these animals towards extinction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:11.280
in recent decades, but this is not the first time&nbsp;
they have faced such a profound threat. Since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:17.440
first days of the horseshoe crab’s ancestor,&nbsp;
they have faced - and survived - all FIVE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:23.440
mass extinctions. These extinction events&nbsp;
are defined as the loss of least 75 percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:30.960
of species, happening in the geological blink&nbsp;
of an eye. Volcanoes erupting, oceans warming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:36.240
ice sheets forming, or oceans acidifying&nbsp;
- the great die-offs result from a perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:41.680
storm of multiple calamities. The horseshoe&nbsp;
crab and its ancestors were one of the few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:47.840
creatures to survive - but if so many things&nbsp;
die, how does life rebound to flourish again?

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:53.200
This is the question that researchers at the&nbsp;
University of Oslo are trying to understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:59.520
and is the focus of the documentary “Breakthrough:&nbsp;
Recovering From Extinction” on CuriosityStream.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:04.800
They are pioneering an investigation&nbsp;
about what survived, and what emerged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:09.920
after the largest mass extinction on&nbsp;
our planet, 252 million years ago..

